************************* Back of Envelope Estimation ************************

When we start designing, we should be having few things like

-no. of users
-api -> QPS
-how much data we store

and if this grows 10x every year  so we need to scale the system accordingly and for that we need this estimation as it directly affects the
-scalalbility
-cost
-storage
-performance

Jeff Dean x google scientist says that back of envelope calculation can help us estimate better.

Back of envelope says , we need to focus on 3 things :
1.Power of 2
2.Latency numbers
3.Availability numbers


Power of 2 :

everything in my pc is stored in format 0 and 1 i.e in bits
in ascai system every character or integer takes some kinds of bits

smallest unit is 1 bit
eg. char -> 1 byte -> 8 bits -> 2 power of 3

similarly 
1kb -> 1k bytes -> 2 power 10
1 MB -> 1mn bytes -> 2 power 20

so on.....

so all data/storage estimation should be done in power of 2

Latency Number :

how fast or slow a system is performing is the latency

1s
1 mili sec -> 10 power -3 sec
1 micro sec -> 10 power -6 sec
1 nano sec -> 10 power -9 sec


all latency numbers should be in these notations.


operation names                 Time
L1 cache reference                0.5 nano sec
L2 cache ref                        0.7 ns
Mutex lock                          100 ns
Main memory                         100ns
compress operation(1k bytes)        10 micro sec
Send 2k bytes(1gbps n/w)            20 micro sec
Disk operation                      10 mili sec

this estimation is of 2010 year


so what we observed
-cache is fastest
-memory is faster than disk
-compressions operation is fast as compared to sending somtheing over network, so we compress first and send that over n/w



Availability numbers :

higher the availability, lesser will be the downtime

i.e if my server is 100% available then i have 0% downtime.

like amazon and google servers say they are 99% available and this is calculated based on SLA i.e service level agreement


Example:
Twitter - QPS & storage requirement estimations

Assumption:
-300 mn monthly active users
-50% of these users , uses twitter daily
-user posting 2posts per day on avg
-10% of these tweets is having some media
-data storage we need to store 5 year old data , till 5 yr old, data >5 we can delete, archive or move to some other cloud storage



how to estimate QPS(query per second)-
DAU - 150 mn
Tweets 2 post each user so -> post/sec? -> 150 * 2/24/3600 -> 3500 posts per second

so QPS = 3500

we also want PQPS - we assume mostly PQPS is twicw of the given QPS
PQPS = 7000
Storage - 

every tweet having a tweet id - 64 bytes
text - 140 bytes
media - 1 MB

total storage -> 150 mn active users * 10% 1MB * 2 -> 30 TB per day

now we need to store 5 years of data so -> 30 * 5 * 365 = 55 perabytes


